examples.question_text:
The diagram records students who study Art ($A$) and play tennis ($T$).
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (7,4);
\draw (2.6,2) circle (1.5);
\draw (4.4,2) circle (1.5);
\node at (1.8,3.1) {$A$};
\node at (5.2,3.1) {$T$};
\node at (1.9,2) {$10$};
\node at (3.5,2) {$8$};
\node at (5.1,2) {$6$};
\node at (6.3,0.5) {$4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
a. How many students study Art only, do both, and do neither?
b. How many students were surveyed?
c. How many study Art? How many study Art or play tennis, or both?
d. A student is chosen at random. Find the probabilities that they do both and that they do not play tennis.

e. A different group of $60$ students was surveyed about Art and tennis. First find the missing Art-only count in this diagram. Then find the probability that a randomly chosen student does not play tennis, as a fraction in simplest form.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (7,4);
\draw (2.6,2) circle (1.5);
\draw (4.4,2) circle (1.5);
\node at (1.8,3.1) {$A$};
\node at (5.2,3.1) {$T$};
\node at (1.9,2) {$?$};
\node at (3.5,2) {$12$};
\node at (5.1,2) {$15$};
\node at (6.3,0.5) {$9$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
a. Art only: $10$. Both: $8$. Neither: $4$.

b. $\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}10+8+6+4\\&=28\end{aligned}$

c. Art includes its own region and the overlap:
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}10+8\\&=18\end{aligned}$
Art or tennis, or both, includes the three regions inside the circles:
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}10+8+6\\&=24\end{aligned}$

d. Use the total surveyed as the denominator.
$\begin{aligned}P(A\cap T)&=\frac{8}{28}\\&=\frac{2}{7}\end{aligned}$
Not playing tennis includes Art only and neither:
$\begin{aligned}P(\overline{T})&=\frac{10+4}{28}\\&=\frac{1}{2}\end{aligned}$

e. The four regions add to $60$. Art only:
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}60-(12+15+9)\\&=24\end{aligned}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (7,4);
\draw (2.6,2) circle (1.5);
\draw (4.4,2) circle (1.5);
\node at (1.8,3.1) {$A$};
\node at (5.2,3.1) {$T$};
\node at (1.9,2) {$24$};
\node at (3.5,2) {$12$};
\node at (5.1,2) {$15$};
\node at (6.3,0.5) {$9$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Not playing tennis includes the recovered Art-only count and neither:
$\begin{aligned}P(\overline{T})&=\frac{24+9}{60}\\&=\frac{33}{60}\\&=\frac{11}{20}\end{aligned}$

Place $9$ in the overlap first.

French only:
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}21-9\\&=12\end{aligned}$
Basketball only:
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}16-9\\&=7\end{aligned}$
Neither:
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}35-(12+9+7)\\&=7\end{aligned}$

